# Duet: Baroque-Style Duo in D minor (repertoire plan: st-duet-baroque). An original minuet for two
# guitars in binary form, 24 bars: the first half (bars 1-8) goes from D minor to F major, the
# relative major; the second half (bars 9-24) moves through C, F, D and G minor to a Phrygian half
# cadence on A (bass B flat to A, bar 16), brings back the opening four bars, and closes in D minor.
# Each half is repeated, as in every Baroque dance.
# You play the upper voice, all in first position: finger 1 on fret 1, 2 on fret 2, 3 on fret 3,
# strings 3 to 1 (A3 up to G4). The teacher plays the bass line, also in first position.
# Counterpoint: on every beat the two voices sound a third or tenth, a sixth, a fifth or an octave
# apart; the only other sounds on a beat are the seventh of A7 in bar 2 (G, which falls to F) and
# passing notes between beats. No parallel fifths or octaves. The two lines rarely share a rhythm:
# where you run in eighths the bass holds a half note, and in bars 3, 7 and 23 it walks in quarters;
# in bar 3 (and 19) it walks down D C B flat to A in tenths with you.
title: Duet: Baroque-Style Duo in D minor
composer: Sixstring Academy
source: Original duet for two guitars, in the style of a Baroque minuet
licence: Original study for Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 5
tags: duet, baroque, minuet, binary form, two parts, d minor
tempo: 108
time: 3/4
key: Dm

